1
00:00:00,267 --> 00:00:12,152
This video shows how to put an animation from Pixodesk 2D Animator into a React app, as a dot Lottie file. It takes one package, one file and one component.

2
00:00:13,400 --> 00:00:15,542
Let's start with the app as it is.

3
00:00:19,667 --> 00:00:22,357
A heading, and nothing that moves yet.

4
00:00:23,600 --> 00:00:29,504
To play dot Lottie files, the app needs the dot Lottie player. Let's install it.

5
00:00:35,800 --> 00:00:39,300
The dot Lottie React player is in, one package.

6
00:00:40,533 --> 00:00:45,915
Now for the animation itself. Let's switch to the editor, where a football is already drawn.

7
00:00:47,433 --> 00:00:50,228
It's a vector drawing, standing still.

8
00:00:51,467 --> 00:00:54,131
To make it move, let's switch on Auto mode.

9
00:00:56,633 --> 00:01:00,134
With Auto mode on, every change becomes a keyframe.

10
00:01:01,367 --> 00:01:04,449
Half a second in, let's drag the ball down to the floor.

11
00:01:07,567 --> 00:01:11,067
A keyframe appears on the timeline, holding the new position.

12
00:01:12,300 --> 00:01:13,946
At one second, let's bring it back up.

13
00:01:17,267 --> 00:01:20,610
Now it falls and rises again, in one second.

14
00:01:21,833 --> 00:01:28,677
A real ball speeds up as it falls, and slows down as it rises. Let's set that easing.

15
00:01:35,800 --> 00:01:40,293
Gravity easing, falling and rising, makes the bounce feel natural.

16
00:01:41,533 --> 00:01:46,967
The animation is ready. Let's save it as a dot Lottie file, into the public folder.

17
00:01:52,100 --> 00:01:55,992
Dot Lottie is the compact, zipped form of a Lottie file.

18
00:01:57,233 --> 00:01:59,036
Now, back to our app.

19
00:02:00,567 --> 00:02:04,250
The animation file is already there, in the public folder.

20
00:02:05,467 --> 00:02:07,400
Let's import the player component.

21
00:02:13,933 --> 00:02:16,676
It comes from the dot Lottie React package.

22
00:02:17,900 --> 00:02:21,479
Then, let's place it on the page, pointing at the file.

23
00:02:29,700 --> 00:02:35,996
Its source is the file's address. Loop repeats it, and autoplay starts it when the page loads.

24
00:02:37,233 --> 00:02:39,663
Let's reload the page, and watch.

25
00:02:44,800 --> 00:02:48,353
There it is. The ball bounces, right inside the React app.

26
00:02:50,400 --> 00:03:00,353
So, one package, one dot Lottie file saved from the editor, and one component. That's all it takes to bring an animation into a React app.
